❯ I want to develop a Linux ALSA driver for a Focusrite Clarett 8Prex Thunderbolt audio interface. I've successfully managed to run a Windows 10 virtual machine inside of a Linux host (the Linux host is my development environment), and I can successfully operate the Clarett 8PreX from the Windows 10 VM using PCIe passthrough. To start, I need help reverse-engineering Focusrite's proprietary Windows kernel driver using clean-room SRE practices. I've already identified a similar reverse-engineering project online (Open Apollo) for a similar Thunderbolt audio interface (Universal Audio Apollo x4). Like the Apollo x4, the Clarett 8PreX's kernel driver is controlled via IOCTLs issued from an intermediate Windows user-space service (i.e., `ControlServer.exe`). All mixer control commands are issued to the Windows service from the 1st party GUI application (i.e., `Focusrite Control.exe`) via TCP packets. Given my current setup and existing knowledge, where is the best place to begin reverse engineering the kernel driver for the Focusrite Clarett 8PreX?

Great project, and your instinct to model it on Open Apollo is sound — that project's core insight applies directly here. Let me give you an opinionated answer, because "where to begin" has a specific best answer that isn't the obvious one.

First, separate the two planes — they are different RE problems

The kernel driver is doing two unrelated jobs, and conflating them is the most common way these projects stall:

1. Control plane — mixer, routing, gain, clock source, pad/phantom, etc. This is the path you described: Focusrite Control.exe →(TCP)→ ControlServer.exe →(IOCTL)→ .sys driver →(PCIe mailbox)→ device firmware. In ALSA terms this becomes your kcontrols.
2. Data plane — actual PCM streaming: PCIe BAR/DMA setup, sample format, channel layout, clock/IRQ cadence. This becomes your ALSA PCM substreams. This path usually does not go through ControlServer at all — the ASIO/audio engine talks to the driver directly.

The control plane is where you start. It's structured message-passing with a stable, narrow interface, and — critically — most of the work may already be done (see below).

The highest-leverage first move: don't reverse-engineer what's already public

The Clarett 8PreX is a Focusrite device, and Focusrite's control protocol is already substantially reverse-engineered and upstream in the Linux kernel — Geoffrey Bennett's scarlett2 driver (sound/usb/mixer_scarlett2.c, plus his extensive protocol notes). It already supports the Clarett USB and Clarett+ 8Pre, which are protocol siblings of your device. The 8PreX is the odd one only because it's Thunderbolt (PCIe) instead of USB.

That strongly suggests the device-side firmware message format on your 8PreX is the same Focusrite protocol family, just carried over a different transport (a PCIe mailbox/BAR region instead of USB vendor-specific control transfers). If that holds, the kernel .sys driver and ControlServer.exe are mostly a transport shim, and the valuable, device-specific intelligence — the actual command/notification message format — is something you can largely lift from existing open documentation.

So before touching a debugger:
- Read the scarlett2 source and Bennett's protocol writeups. Understand the command/notification structure (segments, config space, the request/response framing).
- Frame your RE goal as: "confirm whether the 8PreX speaks the scarlett2 protocol, and discover the PCIe transport that replaces USB." That's a dramatically smaller problem than "reverse a kernel driver from scratch."

The second cheap, high-value move: recon on the Linux host

You already have the device on a Linux host. Before binding it to vfio-pci for passthrough, capture its native PCIe identity — this is the literal foundation of your eventual ALSA driver and costs nothing:

lspci -nnvvv   # vendor:device IDs, subsystem ID, BAR sizes/types, capabilities, MSI/MSI-X

Note the BAR layout especially — the control mailbox and the DMA/streaming registers will live in those BARs, and knowing their sizes/types early shapes everything.

Then — and only then — the dynamic RE: capture the IOCTL boundary

The single most valuable interface to instrument is ControlServer.exe ↔ .sys driver (the IOCTL boundary), not the TCP layer. The TCP layer is just the GUI's transport to the service and is application-private; the IOCTL boundary is the actual driver contract you must replicate. Use the TCP capture only to label what you see at the IOCTL layer.

Concretely:
- Kernel-debug the Windows guest. A VM makes this easy — attach WinDbg over a virtual serial/named-pipe or network KD connection. Set a breakpoint on the driver's IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL dispatch routine and log the IOCTL code + input/output buffers. This is the gold-standard clean-room technique because you're observing the interface and behavior, not copying code.
- A lighter alternative if you don't want full KD: IrpMon (open-source IRP/IOCTL monitor) to passively log IOCTLs to the device stack.
- Correlate by stimulus. Toggle one control in Focusrite Control.exe (e.g., move fader 3, or flip phantom on input 1). Capture simultaneously: the TCP message (Wireshark on loopback) and the resulting IOCTL buffer. The TCP side gives you the human-meaningful label; the IOCTL side gives you the wire bytes. Build a stimulus→bytes table, one control at a time. Cross-reference each captured buffer against the scarlett2 message format — you'll likely find them matching, which both validates your hypothesis and tells you the transport framing.

On clean-room discipline (since you raised it)

For interoperability this is well-supported, but keep the hygiene tight:
- Document facts and interfaces — IOCTL codes, byte offsets, message framing, observed behavior. These aren't copyrightable.
- Do not paste disassembled/decompiled code from the .sys into your driver. If you're doing the strict two-person version, the person reading the binary writes a prose spec; a second person implements from the spec only.
- Keep a provenance log: for each protocol fact, record whether it came from (a) public scarlett2 docs, (b) your own black-box capture, or (c) the disassembly. Facts from (a) and (b) are the safest to build on; lean on those.

Suggested order of operations

1. lspci -nnvvv on the host → record IDs + BAR map.
2. Study scarlett2 source + Bennett's protocol notes → form the "it's the same protocol over PCIe" hypothesis.
3. WinDbg/IrpMon on the guest → capture the ControlServer↔driver IOCTL boundary, one control at a time, correlated with TCP captures.
4. Confirm the message format against scarlett2; identify the PCIe mailbox transport (which BAR, what doorbell/notification mechanism).
5. Defer the data plane (DMA/PCM streaming) until the control plane round-trips — it's the harder problem and benefits from everything you learn first.

Want me to dig deeper on any of these? I can research (a) the exact scarlett2 protocol structure and which Clarett models/quirks are already in-tree, (b) Open Apollo's specific methodology and tooling so you can mirror it, or (c) the concrete WinDbg setup for breakpointing a KMDF/WDM driver's IOCTL dispatch in a VM. Just say which.

