Now we're seeing the live, driver-programmed state. The headline new fact:

What changed now that the guest driver owns the device

- MSI: Enable+ Count=4/4 — the Focusrite driver requested and got all 4 MSI vectors. This is the important new data point. Your ALSA driver should plan for up to 4 distinct interrupt sources, and "figure out what each vector means" is now an explicit RE goal. A likely split is something like: control-mailbox completion, playback period IRQ, capture period IRQ, and a status/async event vector — but don't assume; the trace will tell you which vector fires when.
- DisINTx+ — legacy INTx is masked; the device is purely MSI-driven. So your driver uses pci_alloc_irq_vectors() with PCI_IRQ_MSI (request 4), not a legacy shared IRQ path.
- Mem+ BusMaster+ — memory decode and bus-master DMA are live, confirming the data plane is device-initiated DMA (as expected; the 64 KB BAR is control-only).
- Kernel driver in use: vfio-pci — confirms the passthrough binding, which is exactly the configuration the MMIO-trace technique needs. Everything else (single 64 KB BAR, Gen1 x1, dummy DSN) is unchanged.

Nothing here contradicts the plan — it sharpens it: one 64 KB control BAR + 4 MSI vectors + bus-master DMA. That's the whole hardware contract your driver must reproduce.

Ready to set up the MMIO trace

The technique: disable the BAR mmap fast-path on the vfio-pci device (x-no-mmap=on) so every guest MMIO access to BAR0 traps into QEMU, then enable QEMU's vfio_region_read / vfio_region_write trace events. You toggle one control in Focusrite Control.exe, and you get the exact (offset, size, value, R/W) sequence the driver issues.

What you'll be looking for, so you recognize it in the noise — the classic mailbox round-trip:
1. A burst of writes into one offset window (the request message bytes — diff these against the scarlett2 command format).
2. A single write to a doorbell register (a fixed offset, small value).
3. Either a status-register poll (repeated reads of one offset until a bit flips) or an MSI followed by a read burst from a response window.

Control changes are infrequent, so even with MMIO trapping slowing things down, the control plane traces cleanly — you'll capture a tidy transaction per fader/toggle.

