● User answered Claude's questions:
· How do you launch the Windows VM? → libvirt / virt-manager

Perfect — libvirt it is. Here's the concrete setup. On Fedora, QEMU is built with the "log" trace backend, which is convenient: enabled trace events go straight to the per-domain log at /var/log/libvirt/qemu/<domain>.log, so you don't need a separate trace file or decoder.

1. Edit the domain XML

Run virsh edit <domain> (get the name from virsh list --all). Make three changes:

(a) Add the QEMU command-line namespace to the root <domain> element:

<domain type='kvm' xmlns:qemu='http://libvirt.org/schemas/domain/qemu/1.0'>

(b) Give the passed-through device a stable alias so you can reference it by name. Find the <hostdev> for 09:00.0 and add the <alias> line (user aliases must start with ua-):

<hostdev mode='subsystem' type='pci' managed='yes'>
  <source>
    <address domain='0x0000' bus='0x09' slot='0x00' function='0x0'/>
  </source>
  <alias name='ua-clarett'/>
</hostdev>

(c) Inject the QEMU overrides just before </domain>:

<qemu:commandline>
  <qemu:arg value='-set'/>
  <qemu:arg value='device.ua-clarett.x-no-mmap=on'/>
  <qemu:arg value='-trace'/>
  <qemu:arg value='enable=vfio_region_read'/>
  <qemu:arg value='-trace'/>
  <qemu:arg value='enable=vfio_region_write'/>
</qemu:commandline>

- x-no-mmap=on disables the BAR mmap fast-path on that specific vfio-pci device → every guest CPU access to BAR0 now traps into QEMU instead of hitting mapped memory directly.
- The two -trace enable= lines turn on QEMU's vfio_region_read / vfio_region_write events.

(Optional sanity check before you start: qemu-system-x86_64 -trace help 2>&1 | grep vfio_region to confirm those event names exist on your build.)

2. Capture

sudo tail -f /var/log/libvirt/qemu/<domain>.log | grep vfio_region

Start the VM, then watch. Each line looks roughly like:

vfio_region_read  (0000:09:00.0:region0+0x0040, 4) = 0x00000001
vfio_region_write (0000:09:00.0:region0+0x1004, 4, 0x0000002a)

i.e. region<index> + offset, access size, and the data. Since your device only has BAR0 as MMIO, essentially everything will be region0. (Config-space accesses use different events — vfio_pci_read_config — so they won't pollute this stream.)

Built-in verification that x-no-mmap took effect: if you see vfio_region_* lines for region0 at all, it worked. If the BAR were still mmap'd, CPU accesses would bypass QEMU and these events would never fire. No lines = the -set didn't apply (usually an alias-name mismatch).

3. Methodology

- The boot/init burst is gold, not noise. The big flurry right after the driver loads is the device bring-up sequence — reset, firmware handshake, mailbox init, DMA-ring setup. That's exactly what your driver's probe() will have to replicate, so save it.
- Then change one thing at a time. Let the log go quiet, note the wall-clock time, toggle a single control in Focusrite Control.exe (phantom on input 1, one fader step), note the time again. The delta between those timestamps is that control's transaction. Build an offset → meaning map one control at a time.
- Decode against scarlett2. In each transaction you should see the mailbox pattern: a write burst (the request message — diff these bytes against the scarlett2 command framing), a doorbell write, then a status poll or an MSI followed by a read burst (the response).
- Correlate the 4 MSI vectors. Note which operations are followed by which interrupt-related activity to start pinning down what each of the 4 vectors signals.

Two caveats

- Audio streaming will likely glitch/underrun with mmap disabled — that's expected and fine. The control plane traces perfectly, and for the data plane you only need to capture the setup/teardown register sequence, not clean playback.
- Revert when done. Remove the <qemu:commandline> block (or at least x-no-mmap) to restore native performance once you've captured what you need. The trace volume can also get large if the driver polls a status register in a tight loop — grep/tail filtering keeps it manageable.

Once you've got a capture of even one control toggle, paste a snippet and I'll help you decode the offsets and match the payload against the scarlett2 message format.

