❯ I'll get right on that. But before I do, when you say "scarlett2", do you mean "fcp"?

Good catch — and the fact that you reached for "fcp" tells me you've already been reading the current sources. Let me be precise, because I was using "scarlett2" loosely and the two words name different things:

- scarlett2 = the driver — sound/usb/mixer_scarlett2.c, Geoffrey Bennett's in-kernel, device-specific implementation covering Scarlett Gen 2/3/4, Clarett USB, Clarett+ 8Pre, Vocaster, etc. It hardcodes per-model config tables.
- FCP = Focusrite Control Protocol — Bennett's name for the underlying wire protocol those devices speak. It's also the name of the newer, generic driver (snd-fcp / the FCP kernel module + the userspace fcp-server daemon), which landed around Linux 6.13. The kernel side does minimal protocol transport, and the device-specific configuration logic moves out to userspace fcp-server.

So when I said "diff the payload against the scarlett2 message format," what I really meant was the protocol — which is properly called FCP. You're right to substitute the term.

For your purposes the distinction matters in two practical ways:

1. FCP is the artifact to compare against, not scarlett2's per-device tables. The scarlett2 tables describe which config items a given USB model exposes; the FCP framing describes how commands/notifications are structured on the wire. Your 8PreX captures will match the framing (FCP), while the specific config map will be its own thing you derive from the trace. Don't expect the 8PreX to be byte-identical to any USB model's table — expect the command/response/notification structure to be recognizable.
2. The FCP architecture is a strong model for your own driver. The newer split — thin kernel transport + userspace fcp-server holding device specifics — is almost exactly analogous to your Windows stack (thin .sys + ControlServer.exe). If you can make the 8PreX's PCIe mailbox look like an FCP transport to the kernel, you might be able to ride a lot of the existing FCP userspace tooling (fcp-server, alsa-scarlett-gui) rather than reimplementing the mixer logic. Worth keeping in view as a target architecture even though step one is just decoding the trace.

Two honest caveats on my end:
- This reflects my knowledge as of early 2026 — verify the current module/daemon names and the exact FCP/scarlett2 split against the kernel tree and Bennett's repo before you lean on it.
- Whether the 8PreX's Thunderbolt firmware speaks a protocol generation that lines up cleanly with the USB FCP devices is unknown until you trace it. The Clarett USB models are in scarlett2; the Thunderbolt Claretts never were, and their firmware could predate or diverge from the FCP-era command set. The capture will tell you.

So: read "everything I said about scarlett2's message format" as "FCP." The driver name was the wrong handle; the protocol is what you're matching against.

