❯ Another question: can XML files from an application's install directory be used when clean-room reverse engineering?

Short answer: yes — you can read them and extract the facts they contain, and they're arguably one of your cleaner sources. But they belong on the "dirty" side of the wall, and there's a line between learning from them and copying them. (Standard caveat: I'm not a lawyer; this is clean-room methodology, not legal advice — and if you intend to upstream, the kernel maintainers will care about provenance.)

Why install-dir XML is favorable source material

These files almost certainly describe the hardware interface: channel counts, control inventories, value ranges, control-ID-to-name mappings, maybe routing topology. That's largely factual and functional data — and facts and interface specifications get thin-to-no copyright protection, and extracting interface information for interoperability is the well-supported case (Sega v. Accolade, Sony v. Connectix, Google v. Oracle on the US side; the EU Software Directive Art. 6 decompilation-for-interop right).

In some ways an XML config file is a more defensible source than disassembling the .sys: it isn't executable code, and what you're pulling out — "this device has 26 input channels; control 0x3a is monitor gain with range −127..0" — is hardware fact. For your project it's also a potential Rosetta stone: the MMIO trace gives you the wire format, while the XML may hand you the semantic labels and the complete control inventory the trace alone would take ages to enumerate.

The line to respect

The risk isn't in reading the file — it's in carrying its expression into your work:

- Extract facts, not the file. Document what the XML tells you in your own spec ("input channels = 26", "control IDs → names → ranges" as a table you authored). Do not copy the XML verbatim into your driver, headers, or repo, and don't reproduce its particular structure/arrangement or any descriptive comment text. The data is factual; the selection, arrangement, and prose in the file can carry thin copyright.
- Distribution is a separate, riskier act. Shipping the vendor's XML (or a lightly-transformed copy) with your driver is a different question from reading it on your own machine. Don't bundle it.
- Watch what kind of XML it is. Device/capability descriptors = factual, useful, defensible. UI-resource XML (layouts, copy, artwork descriptors) = creative expression — read it if it helps you understand semantics, but treat it as off-limits for reproduction.

Practical workflow (keeps you defensible)

1. Treat the XML exactly like the binary: examining it puts you on the dirty side.
2. Translate it into a functional spec you author — your own tables/prose describing the interface facts.
3. Keep a provenance log: note that a given fact came from Device.xml (vs. from your MMIO capture vs. from public FCP docs). Facts that you also independently confirm in the trace are the strongest — cross-confirmation both validates the meaning and gives you a clean, observation-derived basis.
4. Implement from your spec, not from the file. Keep the original XML out of the source tree.

The strict two-person clean room would have one person read the XML and write the spec, and a different person implement from the spec only. Solo, you enforce the same separation by discipline: the file informs your spec; your spec — not the file — informs your code.

One more pointer: the EULA may contain an anti-reverse-engineering clause. Interop RE rights override such clauses in the EU; in the US they're more enforceable, so it's worth a glance at the terms you agreed to — but merely reading config data describing hardware you own is about as benign as RE source material gets.

If you want, paste the structure of one of these XML files (with any obviously-creative text stripped) and I'll help you figure out which fields are interface facts worth lifting into your spec versus noise.

