This file is a goldmine, and pulling the USB sibling alongside it answers the deeper question definitively. Here's what the two files together tell you.

Headline: same protocol model, model-specific encodings

Lay the 8PreX next to the Clarett 8Pre USB and the structure is unmistakably the same FCP control model:

- Identical schema and control primitive: every control is (offset-bytes, bits, command) — write a value of bits width at offset-bytes in the device's config space, commit with opcode command.
- Byte-identical notification masks: dim-mute = 0x00200000, monitor = 0x00400000 in both files. Same async-event encoding → same protocol family.
- Same routing/pin model, same pin-h/pin-m sample-rate channel-reduction scheme, same monitoring-preset mapping format, same buffer-size/sample-rate menus, same appspace/flash config-storage concept.

This confirms the hypothesis from a few turns ago: the 8PreX speaks FCP. And critically — the USB Clarett class is already implemented and working in scarlett2. So scarlett2's Clarett code is a verified reference for how to interpret these command/offset/bits fields; the USB XML is effectively documentation of code that already runs in-tree. You decode the meaning there, then apply it to the 8PreX values.

But the encodings are model-specific in every detail — this is the most important practical warning:

