Config-space field map (all offsets are into the device config/app space):

- Per analogue input 1–8: Air → cmd 7, 1 bit @ 174 + (n−1). Mode → cmd 6, 2 bits @ 166 + (n−1), enums Mic=0/Line=1/Inst=2 (Inst only on inputs 1–2).
- Per analogue output (Monitor 1–2, Line 3–10): Gain → cmd 1, 8 bits @ {32,33,36,37,40,41,44,45,48,49} (stride-4 stereo pairs). Per-output HW enable flags (gain/dim/mute) → cmd 3, 1 bit each at the byte/bit pairs in lines 116–172.
- Master monitor: mute → cmd 2, 1 bit @ 24; dim → cmd 2, 1 bit @ 28; gain → cmd 2, 8 bits @ 112.
- S/PDIF: input src → cmd 4, 2 bits @ 132; output src → cmd 4, 2 bits @ 124 (RCA=2, Optical=1).
- Meter source → cmd 8, 8 bits @ 184 (Analogue=1, S/PDIF=2, ADAT1=4, ADAT2=8).
- Standalone → 1 bit @ 188.
- Clock source & sample rate have no offset/command — they're handled by separate dedicated FCP commands, not config-space writes. Flag that as a thing the trace must reveal.
- Notifications from device: dim-mute=0x00200000, monitor=0x00400000.
- appspace: app region at offset 200, 8192-byte persistent store, flashed via cmd 5.

The one conceptual thing to keep straight

These offset-bytes are offsets into the device's logical config/app space — not offsets into your 64 KB MMIO BAR. The two address spaces are different:

- The XML = the config-space semantics: which offset/opcode/value a control maps to.
- The MMIO trace = the transport: how an FCP message (carrying that offset/opcode/value) gets framed into the mailbox, the doorbell write, the response/notification path.

That's exactly why they're complementary, and it makes the trace dramatically easier now: you can predict the payload before you capture it. Toggle "Air on input 1" → you expect an FCP write of value 1, 1 bit, at config offset 174, opcode 7. Find the mailbox transaction in the trace that carries 174 / 7 / 1 and you've simultaneously (a) confirmed the XML is accurate for the Thunderbolt transport and (b) decoded the mailbox framing — what part of the 64 KB BAR holds the opcode, the offset, the length, the data, the doorbell. From there the whole control plane unzips quickly.

Clean-room note

These are interface facts (offsets, opcodes, enum values, channel maps) — the favorable, functional category, and Focusrite's own description of the hardware interface, not disassembled code. Best practice for defensibility: treat this as input to your authored spec (the tables above are a start), keep the original XML out of your driver source tree, and cross-confirm the key fields against your MMIO trace — independent observation of the same facts is the strongest provenance you can have, and it doubles as validation that the Thunderbolt transport didn't change the semantics.

