The parts I expanded beyond the earlier control map:

Pin address space (§3). The key realization decoding the pins: pin numbers are direction-scoped, not global. The same number means different things as a source vs. a destination — e.g. 0x408 is S/PDIF-in as a source but Monitor-Out-1 as a destination, and 0x200–0x20f is ADAT-in or ADAT-out depending on direction. I tabulated both the source and destination pin maps. High-nibble convention: 0x2xx=ADAT, 0x3xx=mixer, 0x4xx=analogue, 0x6xx=PCM, 0x18x=S/PDIF-out.

Mixer (§6). 30 input slots → 16 mix buses (480 coefficients). Important: the <mixer> element carries no offset/opcode, so mix-gain coefficients are not in the linear config space — they're set by a dedicated mixer command. That encoding is a [TRACE]/scarlett2 item, with a working hypothesis that it's 8-bit (the 8PreX uses 8-bit gains everywhere, unlike the USB model's 16-bit).

Routing matrix (§8). 91 / 75 / 67 entries at the low / medium / high sample-rate bands. The reduction is driven by pin-m="0x0" / pin-h="0x0" markers — dominated by ADAT S/MUX (8→4→2 ch per port). I enumerated ~86 destinations (28 hw out + 28 PCM capture + 30 mixer inputs) and flagged honestly that the header says 91, so there are ~5 reserved/extra entries to pin down in the trace. Also included the PCM-capture default-source map from record-outputs, including the loopback pair at 0x60a/0x60b and the per-band index remaps.

